      "id": 1,
      "text": "Fall City got its name from the shallow rapids that made the Snoqualmie River impassable to large dugout canoes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        9,
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://facts.net/world/cities/35-facts-about-fall-city/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Prescott-Harshman House and the Last Frontier Saloon are two historic landmarks that still stand in Fall City.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://facts.net/world/cities/35-facts-about-fall-city/"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "In 1872 the Fall City post office opened, marking the town’s first official federal presence.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fall_City%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Jeremiah \"Jerry\" Borst surveyed and platted Fall City in anticipation of a railroad, but the line was built a mile away in 1889.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fall_City%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Fall City sits at the confluence of the Snoqualmie and Raging Rivers, a spot that sometimes floods in autumn and winter.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        8
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fall_City%2C_Washington"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The town’s first mill opened in the early 1870s at Tokul Creek, just downstream from Snoqualmie Falls, sparking early industry.",
